;; mesh_llm_answer.kotoba -- a mesh guest that ANSWERS with an inference.
;;
;; The three fixtures beside this one all answer out of `kgraph-query`. This
;; one answers out of `llm-infer` (kotoba-core-contracts capability id 225,
;; host import `llm_infer`, ABI (prompt-ptr prompt-len out-ptr out-cap) ->
;; bytes-written | -1) -- the import kotoba-lang/murakumo's murakumo.app.edn
;; names as the reason the itonami cloud-agent keeps every Bot turn OFF the
;; mesh on a JVM resident. The mesh host is this repository's Chicory path,
;; and until `kotoba.mesh.node/dispatch` bound this import it could not serve
;; a guest that declared it: Chicory links by (module, field) and `.build`
;; throws on an unsatisfied import, so such a guest could not even be
;; instantiated, let alone denied.
;;
;; The shape is deliberately the same one `mesh_drama_profile.kotoba` uses,
;; because `dispatch` reads every answer the same way: allocate an output
;; window, hand it to a host import as (out-ptr out-cap), and return the byte
;; count the import wrote. `route.kotoba`'s `answer?` turns that count into
;; the 200/204 decision, so a -1 or a 0 from `llm-infer` is a 204 here for
;; exactly the reason an assert-only guest is: the guest ran and has nothing
;; to answer with.
;;
;; What this guest CANNOT do, and it is the boundary the host relies on: name
;; the model, the endpoint or the credential. Its only argument is the prompt
;; below. All three live inside the `:llm-client` the node was configured
;; with, unnameable and unreachable from here -- which is why `llm-infer`
;; performs no per-call :cap/resource check (see
;; `kotoba.wasm-exec/real-op-effects`), and why the kind-level `:llm/infer`
;; grant in the policy beside this file is the whole boundary.
(ns mesh-llm-answer)

(defn main []
  (let [buf (alloc 512)]
    (llm-infer (str-ptr "Answer in one short sentence: what is a mesh node?")
               (str-len "Answer in one short sentence: what is a mesh node?")
               buf 512)))
